    {
        "content": "<p>I want to \"send Lean objects over the wire\". What's the best way to do this?</p>\n<p>I definitely want correctness proofs.  Also having <code>deriving</code> would be nice.</p>\n<p>I looked at these:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Lean.Data.Json#doc\">docs#Lean.Data.Json</a> (<code>ToJson</code>, <code>FromJson</code>) Has <code>deriving</code> but <strong>no correctness proofs.</strong> (Though maybe one could add some? For example, one could maybe add something like <code>LawfulToJson</code>, <code>LawfulFromJson</code> and  prove <code>(α : Type) [Lean.LawfulToJson α] [Lean.LawfulFromJson α] (a : α) : Lean.LawfulFromJson.fromJson? (Lean.LawfulToJson.toJson a) = .ok a</code>?)</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/oOo0oOo/LeanSerde\">LeanSerde</a> <strong>no correctness proofs</strong>, uses <code>IO</code>, which means adding correctness proofs is also not possible? Also, not updated recently.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/Lean-zh/binary\">Lean-zh/binary</a> <strong>no correctness proofs</strong></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/Lean-zh/protobuf\">Lean-zh/protobuf</a>  <strong>no correctness proofs</strong> and uses protoc, which I don't want.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/yihuang/evm-abi-lean\">yihuang/evm-abi-lean</a> No <code>deriving</code>, also I don't really get what's going on there. Looks less general-purpose and more geared towards some specific cryptocurrency stuff...</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Are these assessments correct? Are there other options? </p>\n<p>I also AI-generated a prototype of ~500 lines, including proofs, which mostly does what I want. Though, it would be somewhat sad if that was really the best way to approach such a ubiquitous problem?</p>",
        "id": 625216222,
        "sender_full_name": "Adomas Baliuka",
        "timestamp": 1789731222
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>A new <code>Std.Json</code> with deriving handlers and proofs is on the standard library roadmap, but it doesn't exist yet. I'm not aware of anything that works today.</p>",
